.TH DUMP 1M
.SH NAME
dump \- incremental file system dump
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dump
[ keys [ arguments ] ] filesystem
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dump
makes an incremental file system dump, on tape or disk, of all files
changed after a certain date.
It is somewhat faster than
.BR tar (1),
but as it
works on entire file systems at once,
.B dump
does not match
.BR tar 's
ease
of use in selectively extracting or saving individual files or directories.
Unlike
.BR tar ,
.B dump
does not provide compatibility or portability to other
versions of UNIX.
.PP
The default output device is floppy drive 0
.RB ( /dev/f0 )
on PRO/VENIX,
RAINBOW/VENIX, and VENIX/11 on Micro PDP-11 systems; it is
magtape drive 0
.RB ( /dev/mt0 )
on other VENIX/11 systems.
This default
may be overridden with the
.B f
modifier (see below).
.PP
The
.I key
argument specifies the date and other options about the dump.
.I key
consists of one or more characters from the following set:
.TP 6
.B a
Normally files larger than 1000 blocks are not incrementally
dumped; this flag forces them to be dumped.
.TP
.B b
The next argument is taken to be the maximum size of the dump
medium in blocks.
If the
.B b
key is not used, a default size is
assumed.
On PRO/VENIX and RAINBOW/VENIX, the
default is the size of a standard diskette (790 and 640 blocks,
respectively); on VENIX/11, it is 24200 blocks (the approximate
size of a 2200 foot, 800 bpi tape \- see
.B s
key).
.TP
.B c
If the medium overflows, increment the last character of its name
and continue on that drive.
(Normally it asks you to change
media).
.TP
.B f
Place the dump on the next argument file instead of the default
device.
.TP
.B i
The dump date is taken from the entry in the file
.B /etc/dtab
corresponding to the last time the file system was dumped with
the
.B u
option.
.TP
.B 0
The dump date is taken as the epoch (beginning of time).
Thus
this option causes an entire file system to be taken.
.TP
.B h
The dump date is some number of hours before the current date.
The number of hours is taken from the next argument in
.IR arguments .
.TP
.B d
The dump date is some number of days before the current date.
The number of days is taken from the next argument in
.IR arguments .
.TP
.B u
The file
.B /etc/dtab
is updated to indicate when this dump was
made, to allow subsequent dumps on the same file system to save
only files changed since this date.
.TP
.B s
The size of the dump magtape is specified in feet (useful for
VENIX/11 only).
The number of feet is taken from the next
argument in
.IR arguments .
It is assumed that there are 11 VENIX
blocks (512 bytes/block) per foot.
.PP
When the specified size is reached on the output media,
.B dump
will
prompt for it to be changed.
The
.BR restor (1)
command is used to restore
dump media.
.SH EXAMPLES
Full dumps should be taken on quiet file systems as follows:
.PP
.RS
.nf
dump 0u /dev/w0.usr
ncheck /dev/w0.usr > nch
.fi
.RE
.PP
The
.BR ncheck (1)
will come in handy in case it is necessary to restore individual
files from this dump.
Incremental dumps should be taken when
desired by:
.IP
dump
.PP
When the incremental dumps get cumbersome, a new complete dump
should be taken.
In this way, a restore requires loading of the complete
dump medium and only the latest incremental medium.
.PP
To dump the entire file system on
.BR /dev/w0.sys :
.IP
dump 0 /dev/w0.sys
.PP
To dump the files on
.B /dev/w0.usr
modified in the last 7 days to floppies
of length 320 blocks on unit
.IR /dev/f1 :
.IP
dump dfb 7 /dev/f1 320 /dev/w0.usr
.PP
Note that the arguments for the keys `dfb' are given in order, following
the keys themselves.
.SH FILES
.nf
.ta 1.2i
/dev/f0	default output device (PRO/VENIX
	  and RAINBOW/VENIX)
/dev/mt0	default output device (VENIX/11)
/etc/dtab	dump record
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
restor(1), tar(1), ``VENIX Maintenance'' in the
.IR "Installation and System Manager's Guide" .
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
If the dump requires more than one medium, it will ask you to change
media.
Reply with a newline when this has been done.
If the first block
on the new medium is not writable, e.g because you forgot the write tab,
you get a chance to fix it.
Generally, however, read or write failures are
fatal.
.sp
